-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.29 (2026-11-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * A processor with x86-64-v3 gets the kernel built for it (ORDER 108; Daniel, 2026-10-05: "no optional, v3"; "The baseline must always stay installed and bootable; optimised parts only add to it"). The plan names linux-image-sparky-stereo-v3 and linux-headers-sparky-stereo-v3 when the loader lists x86-64-v3 as "supported, searched" on amd64, and Hardware Setup installs them with apt. The baseline kernel stays installed and in GRUB (nothing removes it), and the v3 release boots first: /etc/grub.d/10_linux lists releases with version_sort -r, and 7.3.1-stereo3d16-v3 sorts above 7.3.1-stereo3d16 and 7.3.1-rt-stereo3d16 (tests/kernel-v3.sh runs that code from grub-common). The newest build boots first, so v3 stays on top as long as a round publishes the flavours together (one build number). The package depends on no kernel package
  * The person's own choice: sparky-stereo-hardware --kernel base keeps the baseline kernel only on this machine (a mark in /var/lib/sparky-stereo-hardware/kernel-base; nothing installed is removed and the message says how), --kernel v3 undoes it. Every path that gives a different result is an option, one default
  * Packages built for a higher processor level than the machine's are told to the person (sparky-stereo-apt 20261116.1 lets apt choose amd64v3 builds; apt does not look at what is installed): /usr/lib/sparky-stereo-hardware/variant-check reads the loader's levels and dpkg's status for installed packages that carry Architecture-Variant: amd64vN above them, keeps one notice in sparky-stereo-problems (variant-mismatch, LOCAL84 and LOCAL85 in the ten languages) with the Repair sparky-stereo-hardware --variants, and clears it when none is left. It runs after every dpkg run when the status carries such a package or a notice is kept (dpkg's post-invoke hook) and at boot (sparky-stereo-hardware-variant-check.service). The Repair updates the package lists (apt chooses its index by the processor it runs on), installs the packages again so apt takes the baseline build, and checks again; a failed step stops, says why and keeps the notice
  * Tests: tests/detection.py (the plan both ways for v3, v2, baseline, arm64 and the chosen baseline kernel), tests/kernel-v3.sh (GRUB's order, the published tree has both metas and installing removes nothing), tests/variant-check.sh (an amd64v3 build installed by apt on a faked v3 processor, the disk moved to a faked older one, the notice, the Repair, the notice ended; red on 0.1.28, which has no guard), tests/variant-lang.py (ten languages). Not proven: a real processor below v3, a real engine and Repair under polkit, a v3 kernel booted

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Version 0.1.28 (2026-11-16)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  * Broadcom's wl Wi-Fi driver works from the ISO for a card no driver of the kernel drives (ORDER 614; Daniel, 2026-10-05: "Debian's unmodified broadcom-sta-dkms goes on the ISO and is installed only for a matching card, with its licence shown"; found by a desk study of an iMac 21.5-inch Late 2013, whose BCM4360 (14e4:43a0) had no Wi-Fi without a cable). The 802.11ac chips (14e4:43a0, 14e4:43b1, 14e4:4360) name `wl` in share/network-ids.tsv, because the kernel's brcmfmac knows chip 43602 only, brcmsmac binds 802.11 core revisions 17, 23 and 24, and b43's AC-PHY source is built by CONFIG_B43_PHY_AC, which no Kconfig defines (the study's patch, reviewed and kept; the three empty fixture files it lost are recreated). Every wl candidate carries a `licence` line, a card with no kernel driver gets `fact network-no-kernel-driver` and `action broadcom-wl`; the plan still installs nothing itself. The BCM4331, which b43 drives, gets neither
  * The step (lib/broadcom-wl.sh, `sparky-stereo-hardware --broadcom-wl ask|yes|no|repair|reset`; alone it does only this step, with --auto it is the first step of the run): the licence is read from the package file itself (usr/share/doc/broadcom-sta-dkms/copyright, Broadcom's Software License Agreement, unchanged) and shown in the person's language in a window (or on the terminal) with "Install" and "Not now"; only a yes installs. The file is Debian's, never modified or rebuilt: from the ISO's driver pool (/usr/share/sparky-stereo/driver-debs) when it is there, checked against the sum its index recorded, with apt using that folder as its only source (the network is neither needed nor touched); else from the network, with the download's size said before it, and the very file whose licence was read is the file installed. "Not now" is kept for the machine (/var/lib/sparky-stereo-hardware/broadcom-wl-not-now) and the plan window says so with a button to be asked again (`--broadcom-wl reset`); the person's yes is kept (broadcom-wl-accepted) so that Repair continues what was accepted and never a licence that changed
  * Every step is checked after it is done, and a step that fails stops the rest, says what failed, why and what to do (an ERROR line, the end-of-run list, the window) and is kept in the problems engine under sparky-stereo-hardware/broadcom-wl with Repair `sparky-stereo-hardware --broadcom-wl repair`; success clears it: the package is installed (dpkg), DKMS built it for the running kernel, under Secure Boot the module is signed and its key is enrolled (mokutil's own words when it cannot say), the free modules that claim the chip's bus (b44, b43, b43legacy, ssb, brcmsmac, bcma) are unloaded in Debian's order only when loaded, `wl` loads, and a wireless interface of that very card appears. Nothing falls back: a missing pool file, an altered one, a missing licence, missing headers, a failed build, an unsigned module, an unenrolled key, a refused load and no interface are each a message of their own (LOCAL73 to LOCAL83, ten languages, in lang/ and problems-lang/)
  * Tests: tests/detection.py (the iMac fixture and the BCM4331 control), tests/broadcom-wl.sh (every step and failure with doubles, the licence before the install, the file shown is the file installed, ten languages); the real apt, dpkg, dkms and engine with the network cut are in the work's evidence

